/* book Worked example: Q: An inertial system is aligned and then runs unaided. (a) An accelerometer bias of 1 mg acts along a horizontal axis: find the velocity and position errors after 100 s. (b) A gyro bias of 0.01 °/h acts about a level axis: find the position error after 10 minutes from the open-loop formula, and after one hour with the Schuler loop closed. Take g = 9.81 m/s² and R = 6.371 × 10⁶ m.
   1. b = 1 mg = 1 × 10⁻³ × 9.81 = 9.81 × 10⁻³ m/s².
   2. (a) Velocity error = bt = 9.81 × 10⁻³ × 100 = 0.981 m/s.
   3. (a) Position error = ½bt² = 0.5 × 9.81 × 10⁻³ × 100² = 49.05 m. 100 s is far below 13 min, so the open-loop form is right.
   4. (b) ε = 0.01 °/h = 0.01 × 4.848 × 10⁻⁶ = 4.848 × 10⁻⁸ rad/s.
   5. (b) After 10 min (t = 600 s): Δp = gεt³/6 = 9.81 × 4.848 × 10⁻⁸ × 600³ ÷ 6 = 17.1 m. (The Schuler formula gives 16.7 m: at 10 min the two still agree.)
   6. (b) After 1 h: ωst = 1.2409 × 10⁻³ × 3600 = 4.467 rad, and sin 4.467 = -0.9701.
   7. (b) Δp = Rε (t − sin(ωst) ÷ ωs) = 0.3089 × (3600 + 0.9701 ÷ 1.2409 × 10⁻³) = 0.3089 × 4382 = 1353 m.
   8. Compare: the open-loop gεt³/6 at 1 h would give 3698 m, almost three times too much, because it ignores the Schuler loop that stops the tilt growing.
   Answer: (a) 0.981 m/s and 49.05 m after 100 s. (b) about 17 m after 10 min, and about 1.35 km after 1 h with the Schuler loop closed (the open-loop formula would wrongly give 3.7 km). */
