/* book: the chapter, paragraph by paragraph (first sentences):
   - GPS is the United States satellite navigation system; its original programme name is NAVSTAR.
   - Each satellite moves on a Keplerian ellipse described by six elements: semi-major axis a, eccentricity e, inclination i, right ascension of the ascending node Ω, argument of perigee ω, and the satellite's place on the ellipse.
   - T is the orbital period in seconds, a is the semi-major axis in metres and μ = GM = 3.986 × 10¹⁴ m³/s² is the Earth's gravitational parameter (the universal constant G times the Earth's mass).
   - That period is half a sidereal day, so after one sidereal day (the Earth turning once) the satellite has made two orbits and is over the same ground point: the ground track and the sky geometry above a site repeat daily, about four minutes 
   - All satellites share the same carrier frequencies: L1 at 1575.42 MHz, L2 at 1227.60 MHz and L5 at 1176.45 MHz.
   - Two codes ride on L1.
   - The navigation message is a 50 bit/s data stream.
   - ρ is the pseudorange in metres, c = 299 792 458 m/s, treceive is the arrival time read on the receiver clock and ttransmit the transmission time read on the satellite clock.
   - For a satellite at (xs, ys, zs) and a receiver at (x, y, z) the geometric range is r = √((x − xs)² + (y − ys)² + (z − zs)²).
   - The equations are nonlinear, so the receiver linearises about its guess with a geometry matrix H (one row per satellite: the unit line-of-sight vector and a 1 for the clock), solves Δx = (HᵀH)⁻¹ Hᵀ Δρ, and repeats.
*/
